[
    {
        "content": "<p>I have a summer project student, <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"1098946\">@Merle In der Rieden</span> , and we're going to try and put a Riemannian metric on the n-sphere (which will be a lot of fun for me because I have a very shaky understanding of manifolds). </p>\n<p>We've got as far as something which typechecks, but I've been reading all the small print and I am now fairly confident that I've asked Merle to do something impossible.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Geometry</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Manifold</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Riemannian</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Basic</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Geometry</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Manifold</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Instances</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Sphere</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">open</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Module</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Metric</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">open</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">scoped</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Manifold</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">NormedAddCommGroup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">InnerProductSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Fact</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">finrank</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">)]</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Bundle</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">RiemannianBundle</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">sphere</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↦</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">TangentSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">𝓡</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsRiemannianManifold</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">𝓡</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">sphere</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">synth</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">EMetricSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">sphere</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- oops</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>It seems that a Riemannian manifold comes with a specified metric which presumably is supposed to be the metric induced by the Riemannian structure. However, if <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">E</span></span></span></span> is an inner product space, then <code>sphere (0 : E) 1</code> (coerced to a type), the type which Heather put a manifold structure on, already has a metric space structure, coming from the metric space structure on <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">E</span></span></span></span> and <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=instEMetricSpaceSubtype#doc\">docs#instEMetricSpaceSubtype</a> . This presumably is not the right metric, as it will allow travelling through the sphere. </p>\n<p>Any advice?</p>",
        "id": 610079669,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1784030224
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>(I'll try to respond to this very soon, but need to run now - first teaching, then an important meeting.)</p>",
        "id": 610082704,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1784031133
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>You are absolutely right that the distance coming from being a subset is not the geodesic distance. When you say that you're trying to put a Riemannian metric, do you mean just an inner product structure on all the tangent spaces that varies smoothly with the point, or do you mean also the associated distance on the sphere? If it's the former, there is nothing special to do, just define it and prove what you have to prove. If it's the latter, you should use a type synonym.</p>",
        "id": 610082956,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1784031236
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>(if you go for a type synonym, you should probably call it <code>RoundSphere</code>)</p>",
        "id": 610084119,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1784031593
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>(Or <code>GeodesicSphere</code> ?)</p>",
        "id": 610084571,
        "sender_full_name": "Anatole Dedecker",
        "timestamp": 1784031746
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I meant an instance of <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=IsRiemannianManifold#doc\">docs#IsRiemannianManifold</a> . Ok, <code>RoundSphere</code>/<code>GeodesicSphere</code> it is. But I thought type synonyms were banned nowadays? Should we make it a one field structure? If so, do we have a way of pulling back manifold structures along bijections?</p>",
        "id": 610085845,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1784032168
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><code>RoundSphere</code> is really the standard name in the literature (see e.g. <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/round+sphere\">https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/round+sphere</a>).</p>\n<p>Type synonyms are absolutely not banned. Quite the opposite, they work much better with recent Lean versions. Just use <code>deriving</code> to get the manifold structure.</p>",
        "id": 610086867,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1784032476
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The construction I have as a PR gives you a Riemannian metric on any (finite dimensional) manifold. I am pretty sure that it will give the round sphere when applied to the sphere: <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/33714\">https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/33714</a></p>",
        "id": 610095867,
        "sender_full_name": "Dominic Steinitz",
        "timestamp": 1784035060
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"110038\">Kevin Buzzard</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Riemannian.20metric.20on.20the.20n-sphere/near/610079669\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>It seems that a Riemannian manifold comes with a specified metric which presumably is supposed to be the metric induced by the Riemannian structure. However, if <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">E</span></span></span></span> is an inner product space, then <code>sphere (0 : E) 1</code> (coerced to a type), the type which Heather put a manifold structure on, already has a metric space structure, coming from the metric space structure on <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">E</span></span></span></span> and <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=instEMetricSpaceSubtype#doc\">docs#instEMetricSpaceSubtype</a> . This presumably is not the right metric, as it will allow travelling through the sphere. </p>\n<p>Any advice?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This may be an answer</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>As the Mathlib documentation states (<code>Mathlib.Analysis.Normed.Module.FiniteDimension</code>):</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"The fact that all norms are equivalent is not written explicitly, as it would mean having<br>\ntwo norms on a single space, which is not the way type classes work. However, if one has a<br>\nfinite-dimensional vector space <code>E</code> with a norm, and a copy <code>E'</code> of this type with another<br>\nnorm, then the identities from <code>E</code> to <code>E'</code> and from <code>E'</code> to <code>E</code> are continuous thanks to<br>\n<code>LinearMap.continuous_of_finiteDimensional</code>. This gives the desired norm equivalence.\"<br>\n(<a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Analysis/Normed/Module/FiniteDimension.html\">https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Analysis/Normed/Module/FiniteDimension.html</a>)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It is well known that all norms on a finite-dimensional space are equivalent.<br>\nIn Mathlib, making this work requires explicit construction and proof. -/</p>\n</blockquote>",
        "id": 610096904,
        "sender_full_name": "Dominic Steinitz",
        "timestamp": 1784035336
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I'm back on this. I'm having trouble with the type synonym:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Geometry</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Manifold</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Riemannian</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Basic</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Geometry</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Manifold</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Instances</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Sphere</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">NormedAddCommGroup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">InnerProductSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">open</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Metric</span>\n\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">synth</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">EMetricSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">sphere</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- works :-)</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">RoundSphere</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">sphere</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span>\n\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">synth</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">EMetricSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">RoundSphere</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- works :-(</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Is a one-field structure the only way to continue?</p>",
        "id": 611652924,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1784550298
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Your <code>RoundSphere</code> is a <code>Set E</code>, but you should make it a <code>Type _</code> if you want to declare new instances on it.</p>",
        "id": 611655970,
        "sender_full_name": "Julia Markus Himmel",
        "timestamp": 1784551209
    }
]